{
  "dom_id": "step-q2287",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(y)=2x.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(y) & =2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(y)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\ [(x)(\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{dy}}{\\text{d}x})+(1)(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(y))] & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(y) & =2 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =2-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y(2-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(y))}{x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
